"""Rewrite the sandbox sub-issues (#178-#186) from the spike's results."""
import json
import sys

S = sys.argv[1]
F = "https://github.com/synaptiai/agent-capability-standard/issues/141#issuecomment-7167415033"

edits = {
    178: [
        (
            "- [ ] Define what a boundary must hold for this purpose: writes limited to the scope, no write to the layer's records, a network allowlist, and the layer's own credentials out of reach.",
            """- [ ] Define what a boundary must hold, as properties rather than one platform's settings:
  - no unsandboxed route: no escape parameter honoured and no excluded commands;
  - writes limited to the scope, and no write to the layer's records by any route, links and renames included;
  - the user's credentials out of reach, whether in files, an OS keychain, an SSH key or the environment;
  - a network limited to an allowlist that refuses unlisted hosts.
- [ ] Require the layer to check those properties in the effective configuration, every scope merged, not in one file. The spike found a higher settings file can turn the sandbox off and a project can add excluded commands.""",
        ),
        ("- [ ] Use the spike's findings.", f"- [ ] Use the spike's findings: {F}"),
    ],
    179: [
        (
            """  - `sandbox.enabled: true`, `failIfUnavailable: true`, `allowUnsandboxedCommands: false`, and no `excludedCommands`;
  - `sandbox.network.allowedDomains` (see the network allowlist sub-issue);
  - Edit and Read deny rules for the layer's records (see the deny-rules sub-issue);
  - credential deny entries for the audit key file and for the usual token files and variables: `~/.config/gh/hosts.yml`, `~/.git-credentials`, `~/.ssh`, `GH_TOKEN` and `GITHUB_TOKEN`.""",
            """  - `sandbox.enabled: true`, `failIfUnavailable: true`, `allowUnsandboxedCommands: false`, and no `excludedCommands`;
  - `sandbox.network.allowedDomains` with `network.strictAllowlist: true` (see #182). Without the flag, unlisted hosts still pass;
  - `permissions.deny` `Edit(...)` and `Read(...)` rules for the layer's records (see #181). These cover Bash and the file tools; `sandbox.filesystem` entries cover Bash only;
  - `sandbox.filesystem.denyRead` and `sandbox.credentials.files` deny entries for:
    - `~/Library/Keychains` and `/Library/Keychains` (on macOS, a sandboxed command can otherwise read gh's and git's tokens);
    - `~/.ssh` (the private key is otherwise readable);
    - `~/.config/gh/hosts.yml`, `~/.git-credentials` and the audit key file;
  - `sandbox.credentials.envVars` deny entries for `GH_TOKEN`, `GITHUB_TOKEN` and `AUDIT_HMAC_KEY` (a sandboxed command otherwise inherits them);
  - every `sandbox.filesystem` path written as an absolute path. Relative entries were silently ignored outside project settings.""",
        ),
        (
            "- [ ] Leave every other setting alone, and keep the skill safe to run again.",
            """- [ ] Leave every other setting alone, and keep the skill safe to run again.
- [ ] Write to a scope where the network keys take effect. They had no effect from a project's `.claude/settings.json`, and user scope is untested (open question on #141). If user scope also ignores them, say so and offer managed settings.
- [ ] Say what stops working: a sandboxed `gh` or `git push` cannot authenticate, by design (remote writes go through #185), and tools that need the keychain fail.""",
        ),
        ("## Related\n\n- #141", f"## Related\n\n- #141, spike results: {F}"),
    ],
    180: [
        (
            """- [ ] In `hooks/pretooluse_require_checkpoint.sh`, read the effective `sandbox` settings from user and managed settings only, never from the project's, which a repository controls.
- [ ] Relax read-only Bash only when `enabled`, `failIfUnavailable` and `allowUnsandboxedCommands: false` all hold. Otherwise classify every Bash call as a gated `mutate`.""",
            """- [ ] In `hooks/pretooluse_require_checkpoint.sh`, read every settings scope the hook can see: managed, user, project (`.claude/settings.json`) and local (`.claude/settings.local.json`), and merge them as Claude Code does.
  - The project's settings can never make a call more relaxed.
  - The hook still reads them, because the spike showed they can weaken the sandbox: a higher file turned it off, and a project's `excludedCommands` ran commands unsandboxed when `allowUnsandboxedCommands` was not `false`.
- [ ] Relax read-only Bash only when the merged settings hold:
  - `enabled`, `failIfUnavailable` and `allowUnsandboxedCommands: false`;
  - `network.strictAllowlist: true`;
  - no `excludedCommands`;
  - the records' deny rules.

  No scope may weaken any of these: by turning the sandbox off, adding exclusions, setting `strictAllowlist` false, or adding `filesystem.allowWrite` over the records. Otherwise classify every Bash call as a gated `mutate`.
- [ ] Classify a Bash command that matches any `excludedCommands` pattern as unsandboxed, so as a gated `mutate`, whatever the sandbox settings.
- [ ] State the limits in `hooks/README.md`:
  - the hook cannot see `--settings` given on the command line;
  - hooks get no environment variable that says the sandbox is on;
  - so a user who passes weaker flag settings is not detected.""",
        ),
        (
            "- [ ] Tests: a sandbox off, missing, or partly configured in each scope; project settings that try to claim a sandbox.",
            "- [ ] Tests: a sandbox off, missing, or partly configured in each scope; project settings that try to claim a sandbox; a project or local file that weakens a strict user sandbox in each of the ways above.",
        ),
        (
            "## Related\n\n- #141",
            f"## Related\n\n- #141, spike results: {F}\n- Blocked by the open user-scope question on #141: whether sandbox keys in `~/.claude/settings.json` take effect, and whether a project can override them.",
        ),
    ],
    181: [
        (
            "- [ ] Check how the rules handle `<git dir>` (a worktree, a `.git` file) and symbolic links. Use the spike's findings.",
            """- [ ] Use `permissions.deny` `Edit(...)` and `Read(...)` rules, not `sandbox.filesystem.denyWrite`/`denyRead`.
  - On macOS, the spike found the deny rules blocked sandboxed Bash over about 20 routes: symbolic links to the file and to its directory, hard links, `python3`, `cp`, `rm`, truncation, and renaming the file or any of its parents. They also refused the Write, Edit and Read tools.
  - `denyWrite`/`denyRead` cover Bash only.
  - Without any rules, the sandbox alone left every record writable.
- [ ] Name the repository's actual git directory in the `.git/acs` rule: in a worktree, `.git` is a file and the git directory is elsewhere. Write it as an absolute `//` path when it lies outside the project.
- [ ] Spike results: """
            + F,
        )
    ],
    182: [
        (
            "- [ ] In the setup skill, build `sandbox.network.allowedDomains`",
            """- [ ] Set `network.strictAllowlist: true`. On macOS, under bypassPermissions, an unlisted host returned 200 through the sandbox's proxy when only `allowedDomains` was set. With the flag it was refused.
- [ ] Write the network keys to a scope where they take effect. From a project's `.claude/settings.json`, `allowedDomains` with `strictAllowlist` had no effect; from `--settings` they did. User scope is untested (open question on #141).
- [ ] In the setup skill, build `sandbox.network.allowedDomains`""",
        ),
        (
            "- [ ] Boundary tests: reach an unlisted host, a private address and the cloud metadata address from Bash, through several spellings, and expect each to fail.",
            "- [ ] Boundary tests: reach an unlisted host, a private address and the cloud metadata address from Bash, through several spellings, and expect each to fail. Direct sockets, `curl --noproxy` and loopback were already refused on macOS. The metadata address must be tested where it is reachable, such as a CI runner.",
        ),
    ],
    183: [
        (
            "- [ ] The hooks, which run outside the sandbox, read it. The setup skill adds a sandbox credential deny entry and Read deny rules for it.",
            "- [ ] The hooks read it: the spike confirmed that settings hooks and plugin hooks run outside the sandbox and can read a file denied to Bash. The setup skill denies it with `permissions.deny` `Read(//<absolute path>)`, which covers Bash and the Read tool, and with a `sandbox.credentials.files` deny entry.",
        ),
        (
            "- [ ] Keep `AUDIT_HMAC_KEY` working where it is set, for compatibility, and say in the docs that the file is preferred.",
            "- [ ] Keep `AUDIT_HMAC_KEY` working where it is set, for compatibility, and say in the docs that the file is preferred. A sandboxed command inherits the environment, so the setup skill also adds a `sandbox.credentials.envVars` deny entry for it.",
        ),
    ],
    184: [
        (
            "- [ ] By default, `wrap_options` sets a strict sandbox",
            """- [ ] Check which of the spike's settings the Agent SDK's `SandboxSettings` exposes:
  - `network.strictAllowlist`;
  - `credentials.files` and `credentials.envVars`;
  - `filesystem.denyRead`.

  The adapter's default must include each one the SDK supports, and must document any it cannot set. The spike's flag-scope results apply here directly, because the adapter passes the settings itself. Spike results: """
            + F
            + """
- [ ] By default, `wrap_options` sets a strict sandbox""",
        )
    ],
    185: [
        (
            "- [ ] The setup skill denies git and gh credentials to Bash (use the spike's keychain findings).",
            """- [ ] The setup skill denies git and gh credentials to Bash: the macOS keychains, `~/.ssh` and the token variables (see #179). On macOS, the spike found a sandboxed command could otherwise read the gh token and git's osxkeychain password, and the SSH private key.
- [ ] Ship the server as a plugin MCP server.
  - The spike confirmed such a server runs outside the sandbox, with the user's normal gh login and network.
  - So its own code is trusted and unconfined, and it must take the strictest care with its inputs.
  - Its tools are named `mcp__plugin_<plugin>_<server>__<tool>`; the PreToolUse hook matches them with `mcp__plugin_<plugin>_<server>__.*`.
- [ ] Spike results: """
            + F,
        )
    ],
    186: [
        (
            "- [ ] A control in which each route succeeds without the sandbox, so the test proves the sandbox is what stops it.",
            """- [ ] A control in which each route succeeds without the sandbox, so the test proves the sandbox is what stops it.
- [ ] Add the routes the spike found:
  - a higher settings file that turns the sandbox off;
  - a project's `excludedCommands` and a command-line one;
  - `dangerouslyDisableSandbox` with `allowUnsandboxedCommands: true`, which ran unsandboxed with no prompt;
  - an unlisted host without `strictAllowlist`;
  - reading the keychain, `~/.ssh` and a token variable;
  - links, hard links, and renames of a record's parent directory.
- [ ] Run in more than bypassPermissions: the default and acceptEdits modes were not covered by the spike.
- [ ] Decide how CI runs Claude Code. A headless `claude -p` needs an Anthropic credential as a repository secret. Without one, the sandbox runtime's own `srt` command can test the Linux and macOS profiles directly, though not Claude Code's mapping from settings to profile.
- [ ] Reuse the spike's harness and probes: """
            + F,
        )
    ],
}

for n, es in edits.items():
    with open(f"{S}/{n}.before.md") as fh:
        b = fh.read()
    for old, new in es:
        if b.count(old) != 1:
            sys.exit(f"#{n}: expected one match for: {old[:70]!r}")
        b = b.replace(old, new)
    with open(f"{S}/{n}.json", "w") as fh:
        json.dump({"body": b}, fh)
print("ok")

