export const meta = {
  name: 'rfc-0011-os-boundary',
  description: 'Issue #178: draft RFC-0011 and its Standard 1.2.0 amendments, then review them from three lenses and fix',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Understand', detail: 'map the spec/governance/conformance touchpoints and the reference layers' },
    { title: 'Draft', detail: 'write the RFC, the Standard amendments, schema, statements, docs' },
    { title: 'Review', detail: 'normative consistency, security soundness, conformance and tooling' },
    { title: 'Fix', detail: 'apply confirmed findings and rerun the checks' },
  ],
}

const REPO = '/Users/danielbentes/agent-capability-standard'
const SPIKE = 'https://github.com/synaptiai/agent-capability-standard/issues/141#issuecomment-7167415033'

const CONTEXT = `Repository: ${REPO}, branch feat/178-rfc-0011-os-boundary (already checked out; do not switch branches, do not commit, do not push).
Task: GitHub issue #178 (read it: gh api repos/synaptiai/agent-capability-standard/issues/178 --jq .body), part of epic #141 (read its body the same way, and the spike results comment ${SPIKE} via gh api repos/synaptiai/agent-capability-standard/issues/comments/7167415033 --jq .body).
Summary of the change: Standard 1.2.0 is a Candidate. §5.18.3 lets a layer classify a call from its arguments ("Classifiers") and relax it below the tool's worst case. For a shell-like tool that reads command text, which cannot be made sound. RFC-0011 amends 1.2.0 so that:
- a class-SL conformance statement (§10.4) names, for each shell-like tool (a tool whose argument is a program the host runs: a shell, an interpreter, a REPL), the operating-system boundary its calls run under, or "none";
- a classifier MAY relax a call of such a tool below the tool's worst case ONLY when a boundary is declared AND the layer has verified, in the effective host configuration (every settings scope merged, not one file), that the boundary holds for that call; otherwise every call of the tool gets the worst case. A layer without a boundary stays conformant;
- the boundary is defined by properties, not by one platform's settings: no unsandboxed route (no honoured escape parameter, no excluded commands, the call not matching an exclusion), writes limited to the scope and never to the layer's own records by any route (links, renames included), the user's credentials (files, OS keychain, SSH keys, environment) out of reach, network limited to an allowlist that refuses unlisted hosts; and the layer's own decision code (hooks) and any tool servers that run outside the boundary are named in the statement as outside it.
The RFC's status is Draft for now (the maintainer decides when it is Accepted, because the reference layers only conform once issues #180 and #184 land). Write the Standard text on this branch anyway, marked in the RFC as the text it proposes.
House style: plain, literal sentences; state current state, not history; no metaphor. Follow the existing RFCs (spec/RFC-0005..0010) for structure and voice, and GOVERNANCE.md's required sections (Motivation, Alternatives, Backward compatibility, Conformance test updates). CLAUDE.md lists every validator.`

phase('Understand')
const [specMap, implMap] = await parallel([
  () => agent(`${CONTEXT}

Your job: a precise map, not changes. Read and report, with file:line for each item:
1. How RFCs are structured and validated: tools/validate_rfcs.py's exact requirements; the headers, sections and status lines of RFC-0005..0010; how an RFC is referenced from STANDARD-v1.2.0.md, spec/CHANGELOG.md, CHANGELOG.md, spec/ROADMAP.md, the site (site/ routes are automatic for spec/RFC-NNNN-*.md; check site tests that count or list RFCs, e.g. grep -rn "RFC-0010\\|RFC-00" site/src site/test | head).
2. Every place in spec/STANDARD-v1.2.0.md that mentions classifiers, the worst case, shell, Bash, sandbox, or the conformance statement (grep -n), and §5.18.1 record members (is there a "classifier" member?), §10.4, §11 (versioning/compatibility), and the Standard's header listing its RFCs.
3. tests/conformance/calls/schema/statement.schema.json in full; both statements in tests/conformance/calls/statements/; which tests read them (grep -rn statement tests/*.py packages examples --include=*.ts | head -30) and what they assert.
4. spec/CONFORMANCE.md and spec/SECURITY.md sections about classifiers, the Bash classifier, host gaps, §5.5.
5. Anything else that would need updating when §5.18.3 and §10.4 change (docs/, README, docs/integrations/claude_agent_sdk.md, hooks/README.md).
Return a structured markdown map.`, { label: 'understand:spec', phase: 'Understand' }),
