- No transition: @synaptiai/acs-core is not published, so no reader accepts the old \`acs\` key. A tool that carries a bare \`acs\` annotation and no \`ai.synapti/acs\` is simply unannotated (unbound) to the reader; say so where the docs describe reading.
- LAYER_NAME becomes \`@synaptiai/standard-agents-binding\`.
Do every item of the issue's "What to do" for a changed key: ANNOTATION_KEY and doc comments in grounded.ts; the schema description in scripts/gen_standard_agents_binding.py then regenerate (python scripts/gen_standard_agents_binding.py, then --check); the comments in packages/acs-core/src/binding.ts; docs/integrations/standard_agents.md; the binding's tests that write the key literally; record ACS-Q15's answer in docs/research/analysis/standard_agents_mapping/findings_agent_capability_standard.yaml in the form ACS-Q13/ACS-Q14 use; a CHANGELOG.md [Unreleased] entry. Search the whole worktree for other uses: git grep -n "annotations.acs\\|annotations\\['acs'\\]\\|ANNOTATION_KEY\\|'acs'\\|\\"acs\\"\\|@acs/standard-agents-binding" and fix every one that refers to this key or layer name (leave unrelated uses of the string acs alone, and say which you left).
Checks: python scripts/gen_standard_agents_binding.py --check; in packages/acs-core: npm ci, then npm run build:wasm if the Rust wasm target is installed (rustup target list --installed --toolchain 1.99.1; if not, try npm test without it and report what it says), npm run build, npm run check, npm test; then the binding: (cd packages/acs-core && npm run build:ts) and in examples/standard_agents: npm ci, npm run check, npm test; python tools/validate_doc_links.py; any Python test that reads the annotation schema (git grep -ln standard_agents_annotation tests).` },
  { key: '136', wt: '/Users/danielbentes/acs-wt-136', text: `Issue #136 (read it in full: gh api repos/synaptiai/agent-capability-standard/issues/136 --jq .body). The maintainer decided on 2026-11-25 to adopt the four statuses the issue proposes: Candidate Standard, Standard, Supported, Unsupported, with these criteria for leaving Candidate: every RFC that the version rests on is Accepted; the reference validator and executor pass the conformance suites, and the safety layers pass the SL fixtures their statements cover; each open question in its RFCs is answered or has an issue; the CHANGELOG has the release section; and a plugin release tag carries it, named in the ROADMAP's "Plugin X implements Standard Y" sentence. Keep plugin tags and Standard versions apart in the wording (#113). Apply it: 1.2.0 stays Candidate Standard (unreleased); 1.1.0 becomes Standard? NO — read carefully: 1.1.0 was released with plugin 2.0.0 and is the current released version, so it is "Standard" until 1.2.0 is released, at which point it becomes Supported; 1.0.0 is outside the support window ("at least the last two minor versions": 1.1 and 1.0 are the last two released minors, so check whether 1.0.0 is still inside the window today — decide from GOVERNANCE's text and say which you chose and why). Define what the **Date:** line means per status, and make the headers and ROADMAP's Date column agree.
Do every item of the issue's "What to do": GOVERNANCE.md section; the headers of spec/STANDARD-v1.0.0.md, v1.1.0.md (and v1.2.0.md only if its wording must change to match the definitions); spec/ROADMAP.md Standard table; README.md (L12, L522 and badges if they show a status); docs/GROUNDED_AGENCY.md L1158; the site: STATUS_STYLES in site/src/lib/status.ts, glyphs if needed, site/test/plugins.test.ts, governance.ts; and a new test beside tests/test_version_lines.py that each spec/STANDARD-v*.md header status is one GOVERNANCE allows and matches the ROADMAP row. Search for other statements of a Standard's status: git grep -n "Candidate Standard" and fix each that states a version's status.
